Which built-in role should you assign to the managed identity, scoped to the key vault?
Key Vault Secrets User
説明
This answer is incorrect. Key Vault Secrets User grants read access to secret values only and confers no permissions over keys. The signing service would be unable to perform any cryptographic operation.
Key Vault Crypto Officer
説明
This answer is incorrect. Key Vault Crypto Officer includes management operations on keys such as create, import, rotate, and delete. That exceeds what a signing workload needs and violates the stated restriction.
Key Vault Reader
説明
This answer is incorrect. Key Vault Reader allows reading vault and object metadata, such as key names and properties. It does not permit any cryptographic operation, so signing would fail.
回答は正解です
Key Vault Crypto User
説明
This answer is correct. Key Vault Crypto User permits cryptographic operations such as sign, verify, wrap, and unwrap using keys that already exist in the vault. The operations execute inside the vault and the private key material is never returned to the caller, which satisfies the non-exportability requirement.
全体的な説明
Azure Key Vault separates management of key objects from use of those keys, and the built-in roles follow that split. Key Vault Crypto Officer covers lifecycle operations such as creating, importing, rotating, and deleting keys, whereas Key Vault Crypto User covers only the cryptographic operations performed with existing keys. Because those operations run inside the vault boundary, the application receives the signature or the unwrapped key but never the private key itself, which is exactly what the non-exportability policy demands. Secrets and certificate roles apply to different object types and would not grant key operations at all, while the reader role is metadata-only. Scoping the assignment to the individual vault rather than the resource group or subscription keeps the grant as narrow as possible.
