Contoso Ltd. stores confidential contracts in an Azure Storage account container named "contracts". A third-party auditing firm needs read-only access to the blobs in this container for the next 30 days. The security team wants the ability to immediately revoke the auditor's access at any time during that period without regenerating the storage account access keys (which would disrupt other applications using the same keys).

You need to configure access that meets these requirements with minimal administrative overhead.

What should you implement?

Generate an account-level shared access signature (SAS) scoped to the storage account and set the expiry to 30 days.
説明
This answer is incorrect. An account-level SAS grants broad access across multiple services and resources in the storage account, which violates the principle of least privilege since the auditor only needs read access to one container. Additionally, an ad-hoc SAS without a stored access policy cannot be revoked individually without regenerating the account keys, which would disrupt other applications.
Regenerate the storage account key after 30 days and share a new connection string with the auditor for the initial access.
説明
This answer is incorrect. Regenerating the storage account key affects all SAS tokens and applications using that key, causing broader disruption exactly as the scenario says should be avoided. This approach also does not provide fine-grained, immediate revocation specific to the auditor.
回答は正解です
Create a stored access policy on the "contracts" container, then generate a service SAS that references the stored access policy.
説明
This answer is correct. A stored access policy defined on the container lets you set permissions, start time, and expiry centrally, and any SAS tokens issued that reference this policy inherit those settings. Because the policy is stored server-side, you can revoke access instantly at any time by simply modifying or deleting the stored access policy, without needing to regenerate the storage account keys.
Assign the auditor's Microsoft Entra ID account the Storage Blob Data Reader role using Azure RBAC, scoped to the container.
説明
This answer is incorrect. While Azure RBAC with Storage Blob Data Reader is a valid access control method, it requires the auditor to have a Microsoft Entra identity known to the tenant, which is often impractical for external third-party auditors without guest account provisioning. The scenario emphasizes minimal administrative overhead and a simple time-bound revocable mechanism, which a stored access policy handles more directly for external parties.
全体的な説明
Stored access policies provide a server-side mechanism to define constraints (permissions, start/expiry time) for SAS tokens at the container level, enabling administrators to revoke or modify access instantly by updating the policy—without regenerating storage account keys that would impact other consumers. This makes it the ideal solution for granting time-limited, revocable access to external parties like auditors.
